kubeos-storage (1.0.0-1kubeos8) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Remove a stray grep from the postinst. It had no file argument, so it read stdin - the
    terminal in a live-build chroot - and blocked the ISO build for 10 minutes at
    "Setting up kubeos-storage". Caught by watching the build instead of trusting it.

kubeos-storage (1.0.0-1kubeos7) trixie; urgency=medium

  * dh_installsystemd gets --no-enable only, not --no-start. --no-start also stops the
    units during an upgrade and never starts them again: upgrading this package took the
    whole control plane down (all eight units inactive, kubectl refused on :6443), measured
    on the live node on 2026-11-19. --no-enable alone is what was needed - it stops the
    package from enabling units (flanneld above all), while the normal stop/start around a
    binary upgrade keeps the cluster running.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:40:00 +0530

kubeos-storage (1.0.0-1kubeos6) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Stop dh_installsystemd from enabling and starting/restarting units by itself. It
    were enabling flanneld.service - which hook 9050 documents as never-to-enable, since
    it cannot run as a host service - and it restarted units on every upgrade, including
    control-plane units that must not start before node-setup has written the PKI. The
    .wants links shipped in the package now decide, and nothing else.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:20:00 +0530

kubeos-storage (1.0.0-1kubeos5) trixie; urgency=medium

  * kubeos-storage-init detects a damaged thin pool instead of letting the machine find
    out the hard way. A hard power cut can corrupt the LVM thin metadata, and until now
    nothing said so: the node simply failed to mount pod data (and, before nofail, failed
    to boot). It now runs the activation check itself, logs the reason, and writes
    /var/lib/kubeos/storage-damaged for the dashboard.
  * New command: kubeos-storage-repair. It refuses to touch a healthy pool, requires --yes
    because repair can discard thin volumes, rebuilds the metadata from the spare copy,
    then hands over to kubeos-storage-init so the mount and the fstab line end up correct.
    The unrepaired metadata is kept as kubeos-vg/kubeos-data_metaN and the command says so.

kubeos-storage (1.0.0-1kubeos4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The postinst must ignore commented-out fstab lines. On the live node the pod-data
    line had been commented out by hand during recovery and the postinst still edited it
    and reported success, which is worse than doing nothing.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:55:00 +0530

kubeos-storage (1.0.0-1kubeos3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The pod-data fstab line gets nofail, x-systemd.device-timeout=30s and an fsck pass
    of 0. Without nofail, a thin pool that fails to activate - a hard power cut is
    enough - made systemd wait for the device and drop the machine into emergency
    mode, so the node would not boot at all because dynamic storage was unavailable.
    Measured on the live node on 2026-11-19 after a power failure.
  * The postinst repairs an existing /etc/fstab in place: kubeos-storage-init only
    writes that line at first boot, so upgraded nodes would otherwise keep the
    dangerous version.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:20:00 +0530

kubeos-storage (1.0.0-1kubeos2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Ship the unit enablement as a package file. Build hook 9050 called
    systemctl enable for these units, which a package must not do: it aborts a
    chroot build (the trap documented in that hook). A .wants symlink owned by
    dpkg is the same declaration, so a node installed from the archive alone
    comes up identically.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 10:30:00 +0530

